---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Biology's Big Questions">
  <div class="content-wrapper">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">APS Framework</p>

      <h1>Biology's Big Questions</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        The questions that have shaped biology for centuries — and how APS approaches them.
      </p>

      <p class="hero-guidance">
        What is life? What makes something alive? Why do organisms appear goal-directed?
        What are genes, species, evolution, and cognition?
      </p>

      <p class="hero-guidance">
        APS approaches these questions through agency, process, and scale.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="home-section">
      <div class="article-text-block">
        <h2>A Guided Entry Point</h2>

        <p>
          This page gathers the major public-facing questions addressed by the APS Framework.
          It is designed as a guided entry point rather than an alphabetical article index.
        </p>
        <p>
          Although these questions appear very different, APS argues that they are
          connected. Questions about life, agency, development, evolution, cognition,
          and biological organisation can be understood as different ways of examining
          the same underlying phenomenon: the persistence of living systems through time.
        </p>

        <p>
          The APS Framework approaches these questions through three organising ideas:
          agency, process, and scale. Together these provide a way of understanding how
          the major concepts of biology fit together within a common explanatory structure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  
<section class="home-section">
  <h2>Foundations of Biology</h2>

  <ul class="card-list">

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/what-is-life-a-biological-question-revisited/">
            What Is Life?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Most definitions of life rely on lists of properties. APS approaches life
        as viability-oriented, constraint-closed organisation sustained through time.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/how-do-we-know-something-is-alive/">
            How Do We Know Something Is Alive?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Living systems must be distinguished from systems that merely appear alive.
        APS emphasises organisational evidence and persistence.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/why-is-biology-different-from-physics/">
            Why Is Biology Different From Physics?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Biological systems obey physical laws while exhibiting forms of organisation
        that require specifically biological explanation.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/why-do-organisms-have-goals/">
            Why Do Organisms Have Goals?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        APS interprets biological goals through viability-oriented agency rather than
        conscious intention.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/design-in-nature-an-aps-clarification/">
            Is There Design in Nature?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Living systems often appear designed, yet modern biology rejects external
        designers. APS argues that biological design is real, but emerges from
        organised persistence rather than prior intention or planning.
      </p>
    </li>

  </ul>
</section>
<section class="home-section">
  <h2>Evolution and Change</h2>

  <ul class="card-list">

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/what-is-a-gene/">
            What Is a Gene?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Genes are important biological resources, but they are not blueprints,
        instructions, or independent causal agents.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/what-is-a-species-in-aps/">
            What Is a Species?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Species are evolving lineages rather than fixed categories or immutable kinds.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/is-evolution-random/">
            Is Evolution Random?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Evolution involves variation, selection, and organisation. It is neither
        wholly random nor wholly directed.
      </p>
    </li>

  </ul>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <h2>The Boundaries of Life</h2>

  <ul class="card-list">

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/are-viruses-alive/">
            Are Viruses Alive?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Viruses occupy one of biology's most controversial borderlands.
        APS examines whether they participate in viability-oriented
        organised persistence or depend upon it.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="card">
      <div class="card-header">
        <h3 class="card-title">
          <a href="/articles/can-ai-be-alive-an-aps-clarification/">
            Can AI Be Alive?
          </a>
        </h3>
      </div>

      <p class="card-excerpt">
        Behavioural sophistication alone is not sufficient for life.
        APS examines whether artificial systems satisfy the organisational
        requirements associated with living systems.
      </p>
    </li>

  </ul>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <div class="article-text-block">

    <h2>How the Big Questions Connect</h2>

    <p>
      The major questions of biology are often treated as separate problems.
      APS suggests that they are connected.
    </p>

    <p>
      Questions about life lead naturally to questions about agency,
      persistence, development, evolution, and cognition. Rather than
      standing as isolated topics, these concepts can be understood as
      complementary ways of explaining living organisation.
    </p>

  </div>

  <div class="aps-diagram">
    <pre>
What Is Life?
       ↓
What Makes Something Alive?
       ↓
Why Do Organisms Have Goals?
       ↓
How Do Organisms Persist?
       ↓
How Do They Develop?
       ↓
How Do They Evolve?
       ↓
How Do They Think?
    </pre>
  </div>
</section>
</div>
</BaseLayout>